# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-cad-transport-rounds-reals — alfe re-prints every loaded form for AutoCAD/BricsCAD, so real literals arrive rounded (6 significant digits on AutoCAD)
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-alfe-cad-transport-rounds-reals
#+DATE: 2026-10-08

* Symptom

The curve-geometry probe (=autolisp-front-end/tests/scenarios/entities/
curve-geometry-probe.lsp=, run by =alfe --autocad -l= / =alfe --bricscad -l=)
sets, at top level,

#+begin_src lisp
(setq cg-pi/2 1.5707963267948966
      cg-pi/4 0.7853981633974483
      cg-7pi/4 5.497787143782138)
#+end_src

and entmakes an ARC with =(cons 51 cg-pi/2)=. The CAD stored:

- AutoCAD 2022 (job 17026948757, =curve-geometry:probe:autocad:windows=;
  identical on AcCoreConsole, job 17026948758):
  =CURVE arc-0-90 (entget e) ... (50 . 0) (51 . 1.5708)=, and
  =arc-315-45 (entget e) ... (50 . 5.497790000000001) (51 . 0.785398)=.
  The end point of the "quarter" arc is (-0.0000367 9.99999999993 0), its
  length 15.708: every ARC answer of that run is the answer for the
  ROUNDED angles.
- BricsCAD V25 / V26 (jobs 17026948756 / 17026948755): =(51 . 1.5707963267949)=,
  end point x = -3.5e-14 (= 10 cos 1.5707963267949) instead of 6e-16: the
  same loss, at 14 significant digits.

Values the CAD COMPUTES are not affected (the ARC built by =(command "_.ARC"
"_C" ...)= has =(51 . 1.570796326794897)= on both), nor numbers held inside a
STRING the probe reads at run time. Only literals in the loaded file's own
top-level forms are rounded.

* Cause

alfe does not load the =-l= file with the CAD's own LOAD. The runtime's
source loader (=autolisp-source-load-run-body-impl= in
=source/runtime/autolisp-bootstrap.lsp=) reads each top-level form and hands
it to =autolisp-eval-request-form=. The CAD-side definition of that function
emitted by =source/file-protocol.lisp= (the BricsCAD V26 mapcar/lambda
workaround, issues/closed/bricscad-rest-mapcar-lambda-bug.issue) does NOT
eval the form: it RE-PRINTS it with =autolisp-readable-text=, writes the text
to =alfe-eval.lsp= and native-loads that file. =autolisp-readable-text=
renders every non-string atom with =vl-princ-to-string= (=autolisp-render=),
and PRINC of a REAL is 6 significant digits on AutoCAD (1.5708) and 14 on
BricsCAD (1.5707963267949). The digits are lost on the CAD side, in alfe's
runtime, before the CAD's reader sees the text again.

The same path serves =-x= forms (=autolisp-run-eval-file= reads the
expression and calls =autolisp-eval-request-form=) and the REPL, so a real
typed or passed with =-x= is rounded too.

Not affected: =--clautolisp= (direct and subprocess) -- no re-print.

* Evidence still to collect

The second round of the same probe (branch fix-cador-arc-curve-geometry,
=reader= cases) settles it per CAD: =(setq cg-x 1.2345678901234)= at top
level vs =(eval (read "(setq cg-y 1.2345678901234)"))= vs
=(rtos 1.2345678901234 2 16)= inside a string. If this ticket's cause is
right, the first prints 1.23457 on AutoCAD and the other two
1.2345678901234000.

* Proposal

Never re-print source. The loader already holds each form's TEXT
(=alfe--piece= in the read loop; the =-x= file text): write that text to
=alfe-eval.lsp= instead of =(autolisp-readable-text form)=, keeping the
=(setq *AUTOLISP-EVAL-RESULT* ...)= wrapper and the princ normaliser
(apply the normaliser only when the form contains an empty =(princ)= and
fall back to re-printing then -- or make the normaliser textual). Where a
form must be printed (a form built in memory), print reals with full
precision: =(rtos x 1 16)= is reader-valid on both CADs regardless of
DIMZIN / LUPREC, or =vl-prin1-to-string= after setting nothing (measure:
its REAL precision is the question). Test: an alfe scenario that loads a
file with =(setq x 1.2345678901234)= and prints =(rtos x 2 16)=, required
equal to 1.2345678901234000 on every backend; the vendor lanes run it.

Found while measuring cador-curve-length-and-sampling (its 2026-10-08
section notes how the AutoCAD column was compared despite the rounding).

* Resolution (2026-10-08, clautolisp 2.3.12 / alfe 2.3.12)

Implemented on branch fix-alfe-cad-transport-rounds-reals. Stays NEXT until
the vendor probe jobs below have MEASURED it on the runners.

** Every place a form was printed back for the CAD

- =source/file-protocol.lisp=, the emitted run-common.lsp override (the one
  that writes =alfe-eval.lsp=): now =autolisp-eval-request-source (form
  source)=; =autolisp-eval-request-form (form)= calls it with NIL.
  =alfe-request-source-text= writes SOURCE when normalize returned FORM
  itself (EQ: nothing rewritten) AND =(read source)= is EQUAL to FORM (so
  the CAD never evaluates anything but what was read); otherwise it prints
  the (normalised) form with =autolisp-form-source-text= (lossless reals).
- =autolisp-bootstrap.lsp=, the -l read loop
  (=autolisp-source-load-run-body-impl=): passes =(autolisp-first-form-text
  alfe--piece)= -- the form's own text, leading comments dropped. The
  =alfe-load= rewrite path is unchanged (it evals directly and never
  printed anything).
- =autolisp-remote-io.lsp=, the protocol server loop (every -x / --main /
  REPL / (load ...) request alfe sends): =autolisp-protocol-remote-read=
  keeps the request text in =*AUTOLISP_PROTOCOL_REQUEST_TEXT*= and the loop
  calls =autolisp-eval-request-source= with its first form.
- =autolisp-bootstrap.lsp=, the historical batch paths
  =autolisp-run-eval-file= (-x) and =autolisp-run-repl-request= (REPL):
  pass their text too. Without alfe's override the bootstrap's own
  =autolisp-eval-request-source= ignores the text and evals as before.
- The normalisers: =autolisp-normalize-princ-call= (AutoCAD only; the
  identity on &rest hosts) rewrites a 1-arg princ/print/prin1/load, so such
  a form IS printed back -- now losslessly. =alfe-rewrite-form= (alfe-load)
  evaluates its result directly, no printing.
- Left as they are, on purpose: =autolisp-readable-text= /
  =autolisp-stdout-text= (what print/prin1 SHOW, which must match the CAD's
  own print: =(print pi)= is 3.14159 on AutoCAD), the failure report's
  =[form: ...]= and the selftest loop's =FORM= line (diagnostics, not
  source). On the alfe side, =(load ~S)= / =(NAME)= / =(print TEXT)= are
  built from strings, and =%render-autolisp-literal= (the CLI bindings in
  run-common.lsp) never sees a real (a float would print with CL's ~S, e.g.
  1.5d0 -- noted here; no binding carries one).

** Lossless real printing (=autolisp-real-source-text=)

Candidates, shortest first, each CHECKED by reading it back with the CAD's
own reader (a REAL, and = to the value): the plain princ text (keeps 3.0 or
0.5 as written); =(rtos x 1 16)= (17 significant digits, any magnitude);
=(rtos x 2 16)=. RTOS output is normalised against DIMZIN's zero
suppression (".5" -> "0.5", "3" -> "3.0", "1E+00" -> "1.0E+00"), so a real
stays a real. Zero is decided apart: "-0.0" when =(atan x -1.0)= is
negative (= cannot tell the signs apart), else "0.0". When nothing reads
back exactly, the readable candidate with the most digits wins, else the
plain text -- never worse than before. No sysvar is changed.

** The other direction

alfe never parses a real the CAD printed: values come back only as printed
OUTPUT (=print= etc., deliberately with the CAD's own digits) and as status
lines. Nothing to ticket.

** Tests

- =protocol-form-source-text-prints-reals-losslessly= (hosted
  clautolisp-sbcl with =autolisp-render= stubbed to 6 significant digits,
  like AutoCAD's princ): 1.2345678901234, 1.5707963267948966, 1.0e-300,
  1.0e300, -0.0, 3.0, 0.1, -2.5e-7 read back EQL through the clautolisp
  reader; strings / symbols / quote / dotted pairs print as before; the
  DIMZIN normaliser.
- =protocol-real-literals-reach-the-engine-exactly=, on the variadic AND
  the fixed-arity (AutoCAD) shadow paths: a loaded file's literals and an
  -x request's literal arrive exact; the re-printer is called ZERO times
  for them; the form the AutoCAD normaliser rewrites is re-printed and
  still exact (-0.0 keeps its sign); (rtos x 2 16) of the loaded
  1.2345678901234 is 1.2345678901234000. Seen RED against the old printer
  ("A ROUNDED ... E 1.2345700000000000").
- Conformance scenarios
  =tests/scenarios/language/real-literal-precision{,-bricscad,-autocad}.sexp=
  (clautolisp-only / bricscad-only / autocad-only, one fixture).

** Probe (to be played on the runners)

=autolisp-front-end/tests/scenarios/entities/real-transport-probe.lsp=, run
by =scripts/run-real-transport-probe.{sh,ps1}= (family =real-transport= in
=scripts/ci-detect-probe-changes.py=), jobs in =.gitlab/native.yml=:
=real-transport:probe:bricscad:macos=, =real-transport:probe:bricscad:windows=,
=real-transport:probe:autocad:windows=,
=real-transport:probe:accoreconsole:windows=. Per literal (11 cases) and
path (literal / normalised / read / x-request) it prints whether the value
equals the CAD's own =(read "<digits>")=, its type, =(rtos x 2 16)= and
=(rtos x 1 16)=; plus the CAD's princ digits (=REALTX-PRINC=) and what
alfe's printer chose and whether it read back (=REALTX-PRINTER=). Expected
after the fix: every =literal=, =normalised= and =x-request= line =exact=
on all four. The second round of curve-geometry-probe (=reader= cases, MR
!463) answers the same question for =cg-x=.

* Verified on the runners (2026-10-08, MR !466) -- DONE for the source paths

real-transport probe, 11 literals x 4 paths (top-level literal, normaliser-
rewritten form, the CAD's own READ, an -x request):

| target | literal | normalised | read | -x |
|---------------------------------+---------+-------------------+---------+------|
| BricsCAD V26 macOS (17033116727)  | 11/11 exact | 11/11 exact | 11/11 | 1/1 |
| BricsCAD V25 Windows (17033116728) | 11/11 exact | 11/11 exact | 11/11 | 1/1 |
| AutoCAD 2022 GUI (17033116729)     | 11/11 exact | 8/11 exact  | 11/11 | 1/1 |
| AutoCAD accoreconsole (17033116730) | 11/11 exact | 8/11 exact | 11/11 | 1/1 |

Every literal sent as SOURCE (-l forms, -x, REPL) now arrives exact on both
products; before the fix AutoCAD received 6 significant digits. What remains
is only the normaliser-REWRITTEN form on AutoCAD, where alfe must re-print and
AutoCAD's rtos cannot render 17 significant digits nor a 3-digit exponent:

#+begin_example
REALTX b normalised differs REAL 1.570796326794895 1.570796326794895E+00
REALTX i normalised differs REAL 1.797693134862313E+99 1.797693134862313E+99
REALTX j normalised differs REAL 1.234567890123456E+17 1.234567890123456E+17
#+end_example

(pi/2 last digit; DBL_MAX exponent capped at 99; a 17-digit integer-valued
real.) Moved to its own ticket: alfe-normalised-form-reals-autocad.issue.
